  "answer": "Arteries and veins are both types of blood vessels, but they have different structures and functions. Arteries carry blood away from the heart to the body parts, and they have thick, elastic walls that can withstand high pressure. Veins carry blood back to the heart from the body parts, and they have thinner walls. Arteries do not have valves, while veins have valves that prevent the backflow of blood. The blood in arteries is usually oxygenated, except for the pulmonary artery, which carries deoxygenated blood to the lungs. The blood in veins is usually deoxygenated, except for the pulmonary veins, which carry oxygenated blood from the lungs.",
